<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Thierry of Alsace</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1099–1168</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Dietrich of Alsace</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Thierry of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alsace">Alsace</a> was count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a> from 1128 to 1168. A grandson of Count Robert I through his mother, he was backed by the Flemish towns against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-clito">William Clito</a> after the murder of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-good">Charles the Good</a> and became count when Clito died in 1128. He went on several <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusades</a>, by tradition bringing the relic of the Holy Blood to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bruges">Bruges</a>, and his long reign was a time of peace and prosperity.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thierry-of-alsace.jpg" alt="Thierry of Alsace" width="256" height="246" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Olivier De Wree,Lambertus Vossius · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Thierry_of_Flanders.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
